#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "89706c7f-b9a4-5d5d-84e8-2c6df19db4ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-21860" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n$y' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2$,\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′} - 3y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t) \\cdot \\sin(2t - π)} \\\\\nL{y^{′}} - 3L{y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t) \\cdot \\sin(2t - π)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term\n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi)\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - \\frac{π}{2}) \\cdot 2\\sin (2t - π)} &= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot L{2\\sin(2(t + \\frac{π}{2}) - π)} \\\\\n&= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot L{2\\sin (2t)} \\\\\n&= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′}} - 3L{y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t)\\sin (2t - π)} \\\\\n(sY(s) + 2) - 3Y(s) &= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s - 3)Y(s) + 2 &= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s - 3)Y(s) &= -2 + \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\nY(s) &= -\\frac{2}{s - 3} + (4e^{- πs/2})/((s^{2} + 4)(s - 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/id #uuid "07f59403-66ac-531c-81d3-e286231595ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-21861" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' + 4y = u_3(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2$,\nwhere $u_3(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n$Y(s) = -\\dfrac{2}{s+4} + \\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{s^2(s+4)}$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$." :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t)$, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s)$.\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2$, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B$, and $C$.\n\n- Setting $s=0$, we have\n$1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}$.\n- Setting $s=-4$, we have\n$1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}$.\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2$, we have\n$0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}$.\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}\n$$\n\nusing standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t) &= L^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{16s} + \\frac{1}{4s^{2}} + (1)/(16(s + 4))} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{t}{4} + \\frac{1}{16}e^{-4t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$. Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t - 3)f(t - 3) &= u(t - 3)[- \\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{t - 3}{4} + \\frac{1}{16}e^{-4(t - 3)}] \\\\\n&= u(t - 3)(\\frac{1}{16}e^{-4(t - 3)} + \\frac{4t - 13}{16})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement\n\n$$\ny(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t)\n$$\n\nwe identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "6bd8a6e3-ee57-58e1-b770-f6c3bd011d27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228627}] :question/id #uuid "a4f34a7a-fc84-5a59-9569-b602aa08ac47" :question/math-academy-id "q-342263" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ if\n$y^{′} + y = u_{2}(t) \\cdot e^{t - 2}, y(0) = 0$,\nwhere $u_{2}(t) = u(t - 2)$ is the unit step function.$Y(s) =$\n{{field-1}},$s > 1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e60c35-8d8d-5a13-97dd-33e9bc46ce06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 2)} + t - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228630} {:answer/id #uuid "efbcb206-6035-52bd-b93d-6369ab2b4ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 2)} + t - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228631} {:answer/id #uuid "109058cb-c28a-57e7-9f97-59c1ef16fab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 2)} - t - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228632} {:answer/id #uuid "55130b2d-a9fd-5470-9a7c-bc32f8197fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 2)} + t - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228633} {:answer/id #uuid "558906a1-3673-55ae-b96c-64f4be9d5843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 2)} - t + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efbcb206-6035-52bd-b93d-6369ab2b4ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- (t - 2)} + t - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228631} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6adc696-327a-546f-8d61-a233f0cc7e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228629}] :question/id #uuid "2e984ec0-d914-5a7a-8e5e-6f50f20b8724" :question/math-academy-id "q-342314" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} + y = u_{2}(t) \\cdot (t - 2), y(0) = 1$,\nwhere $u_{2}(t) = u(t - 2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ is\n$Y(s) = \\frac{1}{s + 1} + (e^{-2s})/(s^{2}(s + 1))$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = e^{-t} + u(t - 2) \\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$."}]}
